  "correctValue": "23",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>To calculate the vapour pressure of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></math> (w/v) aqueous solution of glucose, we can use Raoult&#39;s law, which states that the vapour pressure of a component in an ideal solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction in the solution.</p><p>Weight of solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p>Weight of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p>Now using formula</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>moles</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>glucose</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>moles</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>water</mo></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mfrac><mn>30</mn><mn>180</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mn>90</mn><mn>18</mn></mfrac></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>90</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>23</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn></math></p></blockquote>",
